# Tables must be explicitly white — never rely on an ancestor for it

Every data table's outer wrapper (the `overflow-x-auto` / bordered card div that
holds the `<table>`) must set `bg-white` **on that same wrapper**, not assume a
distant ancestor happens to be white. The page canvas behind cards is a light
gray (`gray-50`/`gray-100`), so any table wrapper missing `bg-white` shows the
gray canvas bleeding through the table cells and header row — the header
`bg-gray-50` shading and column strokes then blend into the page instead of
standing out.

## Rule

Whenever you write (or refactor) a table wrapper, it MUST include `bg-white`:

```jsx
// Bad — no bg-white on the wrapper; cells blend into the gray page canvas
<div className="overflow-x-auto border border-gray-200 rounded-lg">
  <table>...</table>
</div>

// Good
<div className="overflow-x-auto border border-gray-200 rounded-lg bg-white">
  <table>...</table>
</div>
```

- Prefer the shared `SectionCard` (`src/components/dashboards/SiteHealthDashboard.jsx`)
  when available — it already bakes in `bg-white`.
- If you hand-roll a card (e.g. a "one level in" drill-down view that
  deliberately has no outer white frame around its header, per the
  modal/detail-page pattern), the **table's own** wrapper div still must carry
  `bg-white` even though the page/header above it doesn't.
- This applies to every dashboard: Site Health, AI Search Performance, Prompt
  Tracking, Source Inventory, and any new dashboard/table you add.

## Before finishing any table work

- [ ] Search the table's wrapper div for `bg-white`. If it's missing, add it —
  do not assume a parent card already provides it without checking.
- [ ] Visually confirm the header row (`bg-gray-50`) and column strokes
  (`border-r`/`border-b border-gray-200`) are visibly distinct from the page
  background, not blending into it.
